Common fixture:

```cpp
namespace
{
    struct RecordedClose
    {
        std::atomic<int> count{0};
        std::atomic<uint16_t> code{0};
        std::string reason;
        std::atomic<bool> remote{false};
    };

    struct ServerEvents
    {
        std::atomic<int> messageCount{0};
        std::atomic<int> errorCount{0};
        RecordedClose close;
        std::string lastMessage;
        std::mutex mutex; // protects lastMessage & reason
    };
}
```

Hmm, atomics with strings need care. Keep it simple with mutexes.

Server setup helper: creates server on free port, sets OnClientMessageCallback recording messages/errors/close.

Client: ix::WebSocket with disableAutomaticReconnection.

Wait — important: client automatic reconnection is enabled by default; after cancel-close, the client would auto-reconnect and the server would see a second connection, and the test's close expectations get muddled. So disable it in tests (except the reconnect test where I'll explicitly stop/start).

Test 1: client cancel at first fragment
```cpp
TEST_CASE("SendCancel")
{
  SECTION("client cancels a fragmented send")
  {
    start server (recording)
    client setup, disableAutomaticReconnection
    std::atomic<bool> open{false};
    client close recorder
    client.setOnMessageCallback(...)
    client.start()
    wait for open
    // big incompressible message -> multiple fragments even with PMD off
    std::string big = random string of 200000 bytes
    std::atomic<int> progressCalls{0};
    auto info = client.sendBinary(big, [&](int current, int total){
        progressCalls++;
        return false; // cancel right away
    });
    REQUIRE(info.success == false);
    REQUIRE(info.compressionError == false);
    REQUIRE(progressCalls == 1);
    REQUIRE(client.getReadyState() != ix::ReadyState::Open);
    // further sends fail
    REQUIRE(client.sendText("x").success == false);
    // wait for close event
    wait until clientClose.count == 1 (timeout 5s)
    REQUIRE(clientClose.code == 1000);
    REQUIRE(clientClose.reason == "Send cancelled");
    REQUIRE(clientClose.remote == false);
    REQUIRE(client.bufferedAmount() == 0);
    // server side
    wait until serverClose.count == 1
    REQUIRE(server.messageCount == 0);
    REQUIRE(serverClose.code == 1000);
    REQUIRE(serverClose.reason == "Send cancelled");
    REQUIRE(serverClose.remote == true);
    client.stop(); server.stop();
  }
}
```

Wait — `client.stop()` after connection already closed: stop() joins the thread. The run() thread: after CLOSED, loop: checkConnection (automaticReconnection disabled → breaks since not firstConnectionAttempt), then `if (getReadyState() == Closed) break;` → run() exits. stop() → _stop=true, close(), join. OK.

Hmm — one thing: `client.sendText("x")` after cancel — WebSocket::sendMessage checks isConnected() → state is CLOSING → returns false. ✓.

But careful about a race: right after `client.sendBinary` returns, state is CLOSING (set synchronously in close()). ✓.

Test 2: cancel at last fragment → success.
```cpp
    std::string big = random 70000 bytes; // 2 fragments... wait steps = 70000/32768 = 2
    callback: return current+1 < total; // false only on last
    info.success == true
    server receives full message == big
    client still Open
```

Hmm wait — with 70000 bytes: steps=2, fragments: [0,32768), [32768,70000). Callbacks: (0,2) → true; (1,2) → false but lastStep → not cancelled → success. Server gets full message. ✓.

Test 3: single frame, callback false → success, delivered, Open.

Test 4: empty message, callback false → success, delivered (empty message), Open. Hmm — server echo of empty message: server records messageCount++. Empty string message delivers as Message event with empty str. OK.

Test 5: server-side cancel.
- Server: on client Open... how does server know to send? In setOnConnectionCallback we get the webSocket; spawn a thread that waits for open then sends big with cancelling callback. Or: when client connects, client sends "go"; server on message → sends big with cancel callback from the callback thread. Server WebSocket is blockingSend — sendData will flush... The message is 200KB; client IS reading, so flush completes; cancel at step 0 → close → flush remaining+close frame (client reading) → returns false. Then server Close event, client Close event code 1000 reason "Send cancelled" remote=true (from client perspective remote=true since server initiated).

Client expectations: messageCount == 0 (never a full message), close code 1000, reason "Send cancelled", remote == true.

Wait — client might receive Fragment events; fine.

Also client receives the close → client (ix) echoes close → server completes. ✓.

Test 6: reconnect after cancel.
